### Findings grounded in committed probe evidence (`tests/repro/claims/*.observed`)

| Class | Probe | Observed oracle behavior (v0.69 pin) |
|---|---|---|
| a) c7 | `sharp-label` (+ existing `sharp-escape`) | Unescaped `#` truncates the line at the `#` — **mid-line and even inside quotes** (`{ label: x # y }` and `{ label: "x # y" }` are parse errors); `\#` survives. Hex special case: a 3/6-digit hex token **immediately after the attribute separator** (`:`) is auto-escaped and accepted; the same token elsewhere acts as a comment. |
| b) c9 | `operator-patterns` | A directed pattern is one-or-more unit tokens (`= `, `=`, `- `, `-`, `..-`, `.-`, `.`, `~`) + `>`; **style is determined solely by the last unit** — `..-..-..>` is a *valid dotted edge* (the upstream POD's own "entire pattern must be repeated" claim is contradicted by the code). Arrow-less: `.-`/`..-` valid with a **single** repetition; plain units need ≥2 (`.` and `=` errors at one). |
| c) c6 | `group-syntax` | Colon joins the group NAME (`( G: … )` → group literally named `G:`); a node belongs to exactly one group — a later group reference MOVES it (earlier group empties); nested inner nodes stay out of the outer group (nesting recorded as a `group: A` attribute); anonymous group is named `Group #0`. |
| d) c1 | `anon-reference` (+ `node-unnamed`) | Anonymous nodes ARE named `#N` (odd counter) and are REUSABLE via escaped `[ \#1 ]` (3 nodes, 2 edges — same node); bare `[ #1 ]` is a parse error. |
| e) omissions | `operator-patterns` | Bidirectional operators `<->`, `<=>`, `<.>`, `<~>`, `<- >`, `<.->`, `<..->`, `<= >` map to the same styles bidirectional; a lone left arrow `<--` is a parse error; a missing endpoint on either side (`--> [ b ]`, `[ a ] -->`) is a parse error. |

